5 #edb/ref 17592186499142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499420 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499420 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499420 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499420 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ebb96b46-f989-5842-a663-f156f4929adf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00663209-74b8-5c10-8a0a-2c782390f942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499442 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3bbe131778b11fe61a7e436d3d4729958bb207961ca9faaac91250cf4f2dde0.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+9\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/dfe6902183d348f21d053acce448bd5012d190b6b678f406099ef9d0a2c83374.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ce9d0ba-72fa-5144-a2b0-16cd598fe603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499454 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d34d844ed99307f03ff2454f8916efa91738d7b967f546ef6e516e003d4fc481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f1e7e70-00e5-5263-ad86-e1c271d552fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499467 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q=11.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5218de49-0b02-5048-960b-3418b12d24b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499470 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q=25.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62aefe8f-6088-586d-94c3-d6e202d70a8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499482 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4}=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, and current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RCL circuit are related by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind an equation for the current in the circuit, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "676e6c66-ab4f-5266-9314-934677a34712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499485 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{21}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=A+B \\\\ 2=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3c08141f91eea5b2d2006926de5239f91630de139fae970147d8267d46429392.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d36cf0d0-fde1-5898-8003-f96433332315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499542 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta =30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $32\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f79f0727-9270-5c79-bad1-22894e26808b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499563 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb44fa46fed9427723f2ff5f6fd31011271f411d5dbc58e232bc5b8bbd86607.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n10=32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(10) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =32\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
